The period from 1544 to 1558 covers the first fourteen years of the [[Sinchew Colony]], from its constitution by royal edict to the conclusion of the [[Second Mahik War]]. It saw the colony's population multiply several times over, its territory more than double, and its society settle into the form it retained for the remainder of the colonial period: a landed settler population, an indigenous population in decline, and a labouring population held in slavery.

The period is documented more fully than those before it, because the colonial administration conducted censuses. Returns survive for 1540, 1545, 1550 and 1555, and it is from these that the scale of the [[Second Mahik War]] and of the [[Secret Trade]] has been established.

== Censuses ==

The colonial administration at [[Anping]] began enumerating the population of the western territory in 1540, four years before the colony was constituted, and the practice was continued by the government of the [[Sinchew Colony]] after 1544. Returns were made at intervals of five years.

The returns record the population in four classes: [[Zong]] settlers who were free; Zong settlers held under indenture; the enslaved taken from the indigenous population; and free indigenous people, entered under the term ''thó͘-hoan'' (土番), a class that included the [[Akwesa]] and the [[Hachinawa|Hachina]] as well as the peoples of the interior.

The traffic of the [[Secret Trade]] had not begun in this period. The enslaved population of the colony was therefore of one origin only, the indigenous peoples of the region, and no people from [[Fosia]] were carried to Sinchew before the 1560s.

