The free Zong population increased more than eightfold over the fifteen years, the increase falling after the establishment of the [[General Assembly of Sinchew|General Assembly]] and the land grant system of the late 1540s. The indentured population grew more slowly, and remained a small part of the whole throughout. The free indigenous population fell in every return, the decline being attributable to the wars of [[First Mahik War|1538 to 1542]] and [[Second Mahik War|1549 to 1558]], to the epidemic disease that accompanied both, and to enslavement.

The enslaved population rose fastest of the four classes. Native slaves increased from 776 in 1540 to 8,604 in 1555, the increase falling chiefly in the years of the western war, when captives taken in the campaign were sold to plantation owners in the colony.

== Establishment of the colony ==

{{Main|History of Sinchew (1538-1544)}}

The [[Sinchew Colony]] was constituted by royal edict of 1 June 1544, which took effect in the colony on 11 November of that year. The edict divided the Zong colonial territory along the [[Qingjiang]] river, and the government of the northwestern territory passed to a governor reporting directly to the court at [[Huwei]] rather than to the administration at [[Anping]].

The colony's first governor was [[Tân Bûn-khéng]], who had been the administration's negotiator at the [[Treaty of Anping]] in 1538. He died in office in 1546, and the governorship passed to [[Ông Kok-hong]], who held it for the remainder of the period.

== Land grant system ==

The lands acquired by the colony in the [[First Mahik War]] were distributed from the late 1540s to settlers who had borne the cost of their own passage. An initial grant of 100 acres was made to each such settler, the figure being reduced to 50 acres as land was taken up.

The system cost the government nothing, the land being granted rather than sold, and it produced the increase recorded in the census returns. It attracted the younger sons of merchant houses of Zong and members of dispersed ruling houses, for whom the colony offered land that could not be obtained at home.

== General Assembly ==

{{Main|General Assembly of Sinchew}}

The colony's landowners were given a forum in 1549 under the name of the General Assembly. It was not an elected body and held no power beyond that of advising the administration. Its principal concern was the shortage of land, and the demand for the lands of the western peoples was carried from the Assembly to the colonial government in the year the [[Second Mahik War]] began.

== Economy ==

The colony's climate, tropical monsoon and savanna with a pronounced dry season, supported crops of higher value than those of the neighbouring colony of [[Louyang Colony|Louyang]], which had a drier climate and an agriculture suited to fruit. Sinchew produced sugarcane and tea in the higher ground, and its exports in the period were cash crops, timber and fish.

Labour for the plantations was obtained through the [[Secret Trade]] and, during the war, from captives taken on the western frontier. The traffic was unlawful: the [[Zong Edict of 1500]] prohibited the enslavement of any person within all territories of the Kingdom of Zong, and it extended to the overseas colonies, though it was not enforced there.

The colony became the richer of the two, though the crown continued to spend more on the maintenance of Louyang.

== Second Mahik War ==

{{Main|Second Mahik War}}

The [[Second Mahik War]] was fought from 1549 to 1558 against the [[Salowi]] and [[Chidoha]] peoples of the colony's western frontier, and ended in their defeat and the annexation of their lands.

The war was not authorised by the crown. No forces were sent from the mainland; the colony engaged the [[Sea Wolves]], Sanese mercenaries operating under no sovereign, and obtained 25,000 warriors from the [[Hachinawa|Hahkina state]] in exchange for pork and iron. The [[Societal Assembly]] of Zong was informed of the annexation afterwards and raised no objection to it, and the governor of the colony was honoured for the extension of its territory.

The annexation added about 203,192 km² to a colony of about 167,883 km², taking its extent to about 371,065 km². The loss of life is estimated at some 43,900 killed or enslaved from a pre-war population of about 48,800.

== Evidence for the war ==

No record of the war's casualties was kept, and the events of 1549 to 1558 were not described as a war in contemporary works. The scale of the mortality has been established from the census returns. The method is as follows.

The return of 1550 records 3,184 native slaves and that of 1555 records 8,604, an increase of some 5,400 over five years. That increase cannot be accounted for by natural increase, the condition of the enslaved not descending to their children, so that the children of native slaves were born free. It is attributable to captives taken in the campaign, which was then in its second year.

From that figure, and from the rate at which the free native population was declining in the years before the war, the number of people taken captive has been placed at about 11,400, and the total loss of the two peoples, including those who died, at about 43,900.

The method assumes that the census returns are complete and that the classes were applied consistently between years. Both assumptions have been questioned in the later literature, the returns being the work of the administration whose forces conducted the campaign.

